-- title:
--   Serre's dlog sends Frobenius to Uₚ on q-expansions
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic a prime $p$, let $N \ge 1$, and let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N` be the intermediate field of $K((q))$ (formal Laurent series over $K$) generated over $K$ by the two series `jqModC K` and `jqNModC K N`, assumed to satisfy `IsCurveOver K F` (principal divisors, each place having residue field finite over $K$, and $\Omega_{F/K}$ free of rank one over $F$). Assume moreover the equality $hE$ of $F$ with `modularFunctionFieldFullC K N`, the subfield generated by the divisor expansions `divisorExpansionsC K N`. Let $C$ be an additive endomorphism of $\Omega_{F/K}$ with the three Cartier laws $C(f^p\cdot\omega)=f\cdot C\omega$, $C(df)=0$ and $C(f^{p-1}\,df)=df$ for all $f \in F$, and let $\delta$ be an additive map from the $p$-torsion of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(F/K)$ (degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones, killed by $p$) to $\Omega_{F/K}$ obeying the logarithmic-derivative recipe: whenever a degree-zero divisor $E$ represents the class $y$ and $g \neq 0$ satisfies $p\,E(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v g$ at every place $v$, then $\delta y = g^{-1}\,dg$. Let $x,y$ be $p$-torsion classes such that, after transport along the additive isomorphism of Picard groups `Pic0.congr` induced by the ring isomorphism coming from $hE$, the image of $y$ is `frobeniusPushforwardModL K N p` applied to the image of $x$. Then the $q$-expansion of the differential $\delta y$, taken along the inclusion of $F$ into $K((q))$, equals [`LaurentSeries.heckeU K p`](def/LaurentSeries_HeckeU.html#L22) applied to that of $\delta x$; that is, the $n$-th coefficient of the expansion of $\delta y$ is the $pn$-th coefficient of the expansion of $\delta x$, for every $n \in \mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the compatibility, in characteristic $p$, between the Frobenius push-forward on the $p$-torsion of the Jacobian of the modular curve and the operator $U_p$ on $q$-expansions, the classes being described by Serre's logarithmic-derivative map $\delta$ into differentials. It is used in the study of the $p$-torsion differentials attached to Hecke torsion classes, feeding the pullback statement [`ModularCurve.pullbackAlong_apply_mem_mTorsionDiffOf_of_mem_heckeTorsion_jZero_of_coe_eq_reductionModL`](thm.html#ModularCurve.pullbackAlong_apply_mem_mTorsionDiffOf_of_mem_heckeTorsion_jZero_of_coe_eq_reductionModL).
